WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.483
A known member can be carried through a universal rule that covers it.

2
00:00:04.783 --> 00:00:08.580
Two majorities of the same reference group must also overlap.

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.269
Some museum volunteers are translators.

4
00:00:11.569 --> 00:00:13.233
Every translator is trained.

5
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:17.011
Some supplies at least one volunteer who is a translator.

6
00:00:17.311 --> 00:00:21.599
The all rule applies to that very person, so some volunteers are trained.

7
00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:23.755
We do not need to know which person.

8
00:00:24.055 --> 00:00:31.884
Replacing every translator with most translators would lose the guarantee: the volunteer we found might be among the exceptions.

9
00:00:32.184 --> 00:00:39.053
The reliable connection comes from tracking who is guaranteed to exist and which rule covers that same person.

10
00:00:39.353 --> 00:00:41.551
Arrow shapes alone can conceal a gap.

11
00:00:41.851 --> 00:00:45.605
Identify a witness or subset supplied by some or most.

12
00:00:45.905 --> 00:00:51.452
A universal rule must apply to those exact members before you carry their property forward.

13
00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:54.504
Name the person or group you are carrying forward.

14
00:00:54.804 --> 00:01:05.791
For opposing majority claims, compare how much of the same overlap occupies each denominator; do not infer a group-size comparison merely from the word most appearing twice.
